#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188204 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "4b196d96-a95f-53d6-a0c5-7233de14a401" :question/math-academy-id "e-5445" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3.$ If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1,$ then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+1 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188207 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0eddf8b0-bdd9-55db-9388-d39bd91c24c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7251" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n\n- $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/c0/c09c9b85da2142dda499df51a25870f0d80360fad7f78e501f26c400128bcce6.png) \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188216 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/id #uuid "91885df0-9f1d-5c18-acf1-70259cf7f4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9987" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$ given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, x=y=z\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec4bc02-0df9-5348-9ee4-473b64cacd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472718} {:answer/id #uuid "0b06b982-fb9d-5cf4-b752-4ccd290073ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b06b982-fb9d-5cf4-b752-4ccd290073ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472719} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc239d3-5a4c-5fa5-98b1-768823c16b23" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72dd48fb-940a-538d-8ccc-7a6e5aa609a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472720} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd1213a-a7a4-5890-88d8-8de888db8343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72dd48fb-940a-538d-8ccc-7a6e5aa609a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472720} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff1d1ba-36f4-5b99-8a47-88e5119e65f7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473755} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05116011-77ca-56fd-9a97-eba1d5a68cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472722} {:answer/id #uuid "01638de6-6ede-5e18-812c-b75ad72b29e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05116011-77ca-56fd-9a97-eba1d5a68cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472722} :answer-field/id #uuid "dec578f4-3243-5145-b140-1c5ad943604d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140100" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ * \n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5364440e-35ab-5352-9bd1-0884f1fa53aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365075} {:answer/id #uuid "8d85edbd-a7fc-55ed-a81f-ec06151240f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365076} {:answer/id #uuid "6f432cea-8a38-5640-97df-08aac8c68621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365077} {:answer/id #uuid "5f813b7b-ad83-5cfd-8d75-d5120f30bfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365078} {:answer/id #uuid "3911bb66-4769-5470-b6cf-ec8614901d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5364440e-35ab-5352-9bd1-0884f1fa53aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365075} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b710e0-0e91-5d91-9b48-e0878a4a3818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb42efe7-06d5-5703-9693-8ecd2e6793ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-141426" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}.$ If $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a58cc7-d8df-569e-9b11-77aa6503eca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365082} {:answer/id #uuid "c98745e4-a910-53c6-953f-ad94a707f469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365083} {:answer/id #uuid "effc1519-26ea-5317-b541-492633bbee19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.t