7287} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2f78e9-40f7-51c7-9823-236bb1c047ce" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-38136" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d3f74f-ea69-5ac4-9199-7d4b27f2d25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187621} {:answer/id #uuid "18a97423-b511-500c-b235-781906775875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187622} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1706ec-9604-5fc0-83aa-b23268e3ae66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187623} {:answer/id #uuid "380ecefc-ac12-589a-9301-26da6ea3cc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187624} {:answer/id #uuid "6b393b03-0970-5be9-a7e9-47d2718ec093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187625}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa5a9922-a190-5a9a-a8ec-646dfd8feb20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e472bd-ec33-5a3e-8b54-62a2891a9c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366147} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dc371b-1396-5e61-a5b7-e3d432e28bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366148} {:answer/id #uuid "eca1d3e3-4905-5d15-ac5c-4cc6e7addee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366149} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ff1b0-90ec-51f1-9adc-f5e7ecc391d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366150} {:answer/id #uuid "f218d39c-ce4c-5b79-8c33-1b7543b0b540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ff1b0-90ec-51f1-9adc-f5e7ecc391d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366150} :answer-field/id #uuid "319ca848-ea15-555c-8b75-4d09284cc695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09a30319-539f-5865-a29f-fd523154b963" :question/math-academy-id "q-47468" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,-x+z=0}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $-x+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=x.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9f16ce-fef4-521e-aa19-909f1091f6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187963} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff61651-2e13-54a4-b210-5bed2056dad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187964} {:answer/id #uuid "1c653d92-380c-5b32-bad5-9eabf3045e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187965} {:answer/id #uuid "64f16583-db95-5616-ba6e-96d47b247b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187966} {:answer/id #uuid "77d171d6-6d3d-5964-ba40-67f525bff66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187967}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6340f7a9-a35b-51f1-bf15-ace1f76e6505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187962}] :question/id #uuid "710bfb29-d88f-5f8e-82d2-27ffc4845463" :question/math-academy-id "q-83444" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T: R^{7} → R^{7}$. If $rank(T) = 3$ then $nullity(T) =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33-ce40b9b312ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406812} {:answer/id #uuid "a80e2855-a004-5120-bf2a-4803a51aedf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406821} {:answer/id #uuid "af65c1f2-8ae8-5564-a484-7a9735e719d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406822} {:answer/id #uuid "c038e33b-b13d-5ebb-aa6f-234d43c1d51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406825} {:answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406830} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c728edd-0030-5f84-bc57-b18190d3fb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" :question/math-academy-id "q-83446" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x+y+z=0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ -x-y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c91297-c7c0-5bdc-b9d9-a9a52ae806c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187969} {:answer/id #uuid "99b215f4-c062-59cc-b003-ea6ef57c1e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187970} {:answer/id #uuid "b7165ef5-c1ee-52e1-97ed-92ea885bd55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187971} {:answer/id #uuid "0874d691-cb56-565c-9d14-cf75209b28ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187972} {:answer/id #uuid "22fb8de2-7841-5738-b5c1-945f57d8818f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187973}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e4fdbb-a7f0-534a-a976-d0c43448ef20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187968}] :question/id #uuid "f05e62e2-1597-5312-8f1a-20a85ca4f47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84059" :question/problem "Find $rank(T)$ given that $T: R^{4} → R^{4}$ and\n$Ker(T) = {[x, y, z, w]^{T}: x + y + z = 0, 2x - y - w = 0}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f285c5-f28d-52d7-8edb-dc3cd9308f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451845} {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bbe03ec-cd1a-5f9e-bdaa-621344b8730a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2821d1-dbdf-57db-8669-559772eabf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451847} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecdec4e8-476e-5e64-bfe1-002886cf49b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26320c-f5fc-5d22-962b-bc6fcc8e7c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451849} {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850} :answer-field/id #uuid "38dc4bf6-46bb-5bfd-8850-b313313b3744" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c81c0d34-9745-5e75-9229-e0ad0c633344" :question/math-academy-id "q-84063" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- **II.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0,x+z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- **III.** If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
